Yesterday I tried setting Nethack 3.1.3 on my PC-clone at home, where I
have a 286 with 640K memory and lots and lots of hard drive.  Unfortunately,
the game still seems slow -- it took me a half hour to clear the first two
levels of the dungeon.

It seems part of the problem is that the game kept accessing my hard drive
every time I took an action.  Even when I was crossing an empty room.
I even tried it with no pet on the level, !time, and at times, there were
even no living monsters.

I realize that the entire game doesn't fit in 640K any more, but shouldn't
there at least be a reasonable kernel that can run all by itself, without
using the hard drive?  I think poor allocation regarding what is resident
in memory is a problem here.

At least, the following should be available in memory all the time:
   front-end for the command interface
   code for player moving and monsters moving
   ordinary swing-for-swing combat (uhitm, mhitu)
   opening doors and chests
   the map info for the current level
   tables for common monsters and equipment

I don't see why all this stuff can't fit in less than 640K.

With just an EXE file, I naturally can't see how the overlay people
did their job, but here's a few guesses on what might be going on:

Do you have all of permonst sitting in memory?  Not every first level
character needs to know about Dispater or Lord Surtur, etc.  I estimate
that the memory requirements for >200 monsters is >70K all by itself.
Perhaps we only need to hold info for 20 or so.

Similarly, are all the artifacts loaded into memory?  Or perhaps,
the entire list of possible game objects, including the rare ones?

Maybe if we had more room in memory for executable code, the
game might speed up a little.  In the meantime, I'll stay with Unix.

Roger Christman


